{[%csl Ga6][%cal Gb5a6,Gh1a8] Pin is one of the most common motifs in chess. A
pin is a situation brought on by an attacking piece in which a defending piece
cannot move without exposing a more valuable defending piece on its other side
to capture by the attacking piece. In this current position the b7 pawn is
pinned by the bishop on h1 as it cannot leave its position because the king on
a8 remains under attack. Therefore White can deliver mate in one move by
playing} 1. Qa6# {The king has no square to escape and the pawn is pinned.} *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Mate using the pin"]
[Black "sample 2"]
[Result "*"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "7k/6bp/8/1r2B2K/7P/8/8/5R2 w - - 0 1"]
[PlyCount "1"]
[SourceDate "2018.03.29"]

{[%csl Gf8][%cal Gf1f8,Ge5h8] The e5 bishop is pinned now by the rook on b5 as
it cannot move because the h5 king remains unprotected. Black attacks the
bishop with two pieces and it seems like he is going to win it. However, the
g7 bishop is also pinned by the e5 bishop as the king on h8 gets under attack.
White can make use of it by playing} 1. Rf8# {Checkmate! In this chapter, you
need to deliver similar checkmates in one move making use of a pin in more and
more difficult positions.} *

